WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:13.350
Singer: O say can you see, by the dawn’s
early light,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:25.130
What so proudly we hailed at the twilight’s
last gleaming,

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:34.350
Whose broad stripes and bright stars through
the perilous fight,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:43.450
O’er the ramparts we watched, were so gallantly
streaming?

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:56.579
And the rockets’ red glare, the bombs bursting
in air,

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:09.290
Gave proof through the night that our flag
was still there;

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:27.760
O say does that star-spangled banner yet wave
O’er the land of the free and the home of

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:37.780
the brave!

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.760
Announcer: Please be seated.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:03.120
Ladies and gentlemen, please welcome AEI President
Robert Doar.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.500
Robert Doar: Good evening everyone.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:18.819
I’m Robert Doar, president of AEI.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:23.749
And on behalf of our chairman, Dan D’Aniello,
and the entire AEI community, I welcome you

00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:25.340
to our Annual Dinner.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.760
We’re so glad you could join us this evening
when we have the privilege of celebrating

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:42.209
two great Americans: Irving Kristol and Ambassador
Nikki Haley.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:50.660
Now I have to say, I begin tonight remembering
the best mass I ever attended.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:57.109
It was the one where Monsignor DeVaney of
St. Charles Borromeo on Sydney Place in Brooklyn

00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:03.559
said, “I’m going to keep this homily short
today because there’s a game on TV I want

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:07.169
to see.”

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:14.349
And those of you who have been with us before
know that it has been our practice in the

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:20.079
past at this point in the program to invite
Bill Kristol up to say a few words about Irving

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:26.689
Kristol, the Irving Kristol Award, and to
extend greetings from his mother, Bea Himmelfarb,

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:27.840
and their family.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.279
But Bea and Bill could not be here tonight,
though they still send warm greetings and

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:39.599
good wishes to all of us and to Ambassador
Haley, and I know we send the same to them.

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:44.799
Speaking of Bill reminds me of the story all
newcomers to AEI are told early in their tenure.

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:50.340
It’s kind of a right of passage, and it’s
an important story, one we hold close in our

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.340
hearts.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.109
And it goes like this.

00:03:53.109 --> 00:03:58.469
In all the years, there was only one time
when Irving Kristol and Bill Kristol appeared

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:01.449
together on a panel at AEI.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:05.200
Father and son were seated on the stage with
a moderator between them.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.200
And the moderator lobbed questions to each
of them, one to Irving and one to Bill about

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.669
their respective careers and views on our
country.

00:04:13.669 --> 00:04:18.730
And all was going fine until it was the final
question.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:24.630
The moderator asked Bill, “What was the
best advice you ever got?”

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.340
And Bill was stumped.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.710
He didn’t know what to say.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.280
So much advice, which bit of knowledge to
pick?

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.699
What was the best advice?

00:04:35.699 --> 00:04:44.720
And then Irving leaned into his microphone
and said, “Honor thy father.”

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.460
So during my first few months as president
of AEI, Irving’s words about people in my

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.760
position have provided a kind of comfort.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:59.090
“A think tank president,” said Irving,
“is someone who speaks with authority about

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.770
subjects in which he has no particular competence.”

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:09.759
Now, thankfully, at AEI, I am surrounded by
scholars who have exceptional competence when

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:13.450
it comes to confronting our nation’s greatest
challenges.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.689
And many of them are here tonight.

00:05:15.689 --> 00:05:21.320
And I want to start by recognizing and thanking
AEI scholars, who are the heart and soul of

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.289
our institution.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:28.650
Many of these scholars have decades of experience
on the front lines of public policy: shaping

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:34.879
it, implementing it, seeing how good public
policy can lift people up and how bad policy

00:05:34.879 --> 00:05:36.120
can tear them down.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.610
And of course, Irving Kristol had a very different
kind of frontline experience.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:47.400
He described his time in the Army during World
War II as deeply formative.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.630
When he joined, he was a socialist.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.260
His regiment was full of tough guys from the
south side of Chicago.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:59.499
And by the end of his time with these competitive,
less than angelic individualists, he was pretty

00:05:59.499 --> 00:06:04.910
sure socialism would never work in America.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:10.310
That wouldn’t be the last time Irving was
mugged by reality.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.600
In the half century that followed, Irving
went on to become one of the greatest scholars

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.080
of his generation.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.770
He shaped public debate and defined the core
values that we still fight for every day.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.620
Writing about everything from welfare and
religion to Joseph McCarthy and the sexual

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:31.729
revolution, he never lost his dedication to
his country and its promise.

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:37.979
His political principles live on in AEI’s
work, including economic freedom, American

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:42.770
global leadership, and equality of opportunity.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:47.689
Equality of opportunity, not equality of outcome.

00:06:47.689 --> 00:06:54.349
This idea was crystallized for me when I first
read Irving’s essay, “What Is a Neoconservative?”

00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:58.530
And he brought the point home for me at an
AEI gathering some years later when he said

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:02.139
he especially did not like equality in sports.

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:06.879
Now, as a great sports fan myself, I love
that reference to sports.

00:07:06.879 --> 00:07:12.789
And for those Nationals fans here tonight,
it must have some significance.

00:07:12.789 --> 00:07:16.749
What would having your team play in the World
Series mean if all outcomes were equal?

00:07:16.749 --> 00:07:23.360
Why would anyone strive to do great things
without a chance for great success?

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.370
And why should we deny the fruits of success
to people who have earned them?

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:35.150
One of my Brooklyn schoolteachers once described
to me the joy he felt on the night in 1938

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:39.629
when Max Schmeling, the German boxer who had
been a favorite of Hitler and a darling of

00:07:39.629 --> 00:07:43.360
the Nazi Party, came to New York to fight
Joe Louis.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.870
Louis, the heavyweight from Alabama, known
as the “Brown Bomber,” knocked him out

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:50.930
in less than three minutes.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:54.449
Given a chance for a fair fight, Louis proved
himself.

00:07:54.449 --> 00:08:03.099
Now there is an example of equal opportunity
leading to great unequal results.

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:07.069
Irving also had a deep understanding of the
connection between politics and the human

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:08.240
good.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.099
He hoped that one day we’d be able to bring
our public and private lives back together

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:19.129
into what he called a congenial relationship,
where our politics supported family, religion,

00:08:19.129 --> 00:08:21.090
and community life.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:26.840
A great admiration for Irving’s legacy on
this front is one reason why we’ve created

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:31.660
the new Social, Cultural, and Constitutional
Studies division at AEI.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:36.699
I am confident that Irving, who loved America
so deeply, would be proud of our efforts to

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:39.539
invigorate this component of AEI’s work.

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:45.180
It’s also why we brought the great policy
journal National Affairs, which I know will

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:49.880
continue to be the intellectual air to Irving’s
Public Interest.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:56.449
And Irving’s lessons in foreign policy also
inform us today.

00:08:56.449 --> 00:09:01.339
While Pete Seeger was singing about flowers,
Irving was asking “Where have all the gunboats

00:09:01.339 --> 00:09:02.339
gone?”

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:08.720
He was never afraid to promote his own pragmatic
sense of world affairs, including a firm commitment

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.540
to American allies and a dedication to American
global leadership.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:19.410
That’s why he often lamented what he called
the ironies of neo-isolationism and the mistakes

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.779
of those who would see America withdraw from
the world stage.

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:29.540
In Irving’s mind, America’s role in the
world required a careful balancing of prudence

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:36.389
and restraint with a willingness to act even
unilaterally when necessary, and he thought

00:09:36.389 --> 00:09:40.680
these concerns about America’s spirit and
America’s role in the world were connected.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.830
“If the United States is to gain the respect
of world opinion,” Irvin wrote in 1975,

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:51.850
“it first has to demonstrate that it respects
itself: its own institutions, its own way

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:57.040
of life, the political and social philosophy
that is the basis of its institutions and

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:59.060
its way of life.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:05.980
To persuade the world, we have to show the
world that we love our country.”

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:11.290
That line of thinking helps us see why our
honoree tonight, Ambassador Nikki Haley, is

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:16.370
an ideal person to receive the Irving Kristol
Award.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:21.440
During her time at the United Nations, Ambassador
Haley embodied this idea that a constructive

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.120
role for America in the world begins with
self-respect.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.680
She is unafraid to stand up for American ideals
and American interests, and she knows how

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.230
closely connected the two always are.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:40.230
It was so refreshing and reassuring to hear
her press this point at the UN, and I trust

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:45.790
she will continue to be a powerful advocate
for what is best about our country in the

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.610
years to come.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:53.279
And like Irving Kristol, Ambassador Haley
has always been a staunch defender of religious

00:10:53.279 --> 00:10:57.940
freedom and the role of faith at home and
abroad.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:03.790
One of America’s greatest graces is its
welcoming of religious minorities and appreciation

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:07.139
for the role of faith in an individual’s
life.

00:11:07.139 --> 00:11:10.890
And Ambassador Haley’s own history proves
that.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.449
Her parents immigrated to America from Amritsar,
India.

00:11:14.449 --> 00:11:18.860
They were teachers, her father in a historically
black college and her mother in the local

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.300
public schools.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:28.110
In their new home in Bamberg, South Carolina,
the Haley family thrived there, and her success

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:34.400
proves that diverse practices and perspectives
only enrich American public life.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.740
Regardless of whether you’re Christian,
Sikh, Jewish, or Muslim, a person of any faith

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.730
or of none, American life is open to you.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:48.709
If you live by our laws, contribute to our
society, and bring the best of your traditions

00:11:48.709 --> 00:12:04.310
to the work of building our country, you will
be an American.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.540
Ambassador Haley’s story affirms that American
ethos.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:14.100
To follow her career is to see the great example
she has set at home and around the world.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:19.800
She has been an eloquent and powerful advocate
for America on the world stage as ambassador

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:25.670
to the United Nations, an impassioned defender
of limited government as governor of South

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:32.529
Carolina, and a powerful voice of moral clarity
in our national political climate.

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:38.269
Whether it’s vulnerable people in our home
state or persecuted dissidents in China or

00:12:38.269 --> 00:12:44.019
Russia, Nikki Haley has provided a sympathetic
ear and a strong voice.

00:12:44.019 --> 00:12:49.569
And if Irving could be here tonight, he might
offer one final reminder for all of us.

00:12:49.569 --> 00:12:56.040
He wrote that “America is unique among nations
in being founded not on race, not on kinship,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.360
not on language, not on religion, but on political
values.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:06.709
The essence of being an American is not your
party, class, or creed, not your race or your

00:13:06.709 --> 00:13:12.800
religion, but rather your willingness to share
in a common set of principles.”

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:19.709
Ambassador Haley, a daughter of South Carolina,
is the perfect exemplar.

00:13:19.709 --> 00:13:25.089
Irving would be proud of anyone in public
life who has set such an example of courage,

00:13:25.089 --> 00:13:27.620
decency, and patriotism.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:33.709
Our Irving Kristol Award is given annually
to recognize individuals’ intellectual and

00:13:33.709 --> 00:13:40.420
practical contributions to government policy,
social welfare, or political understanding.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:46.100
But it’s all too rare to find someone like
Nikki Haley who has done so much in all three

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.420
fields.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:52.020
So please join me in applauding Ambassador
Haley as we honor her tonight.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:12.980
Nikki Haley: Thank you.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:14.080
Thank you so much.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.160
That’s so nice.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.180
Thank you.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:18.960
Thank you.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:22.260
Thank you.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:23.020
I appreciate it.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:24.160
Thank you.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:28.840
Thank you, Robert.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:33.480
Congratulations on taking leadership of this
amazing institution.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:40.020
I want to thank you, your predecessor, Arthur
Brooks, and the entire AEI family for honoring

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.519
me with this award.

00:14:41.519 --> 00:14:45.060
It’s humbling to stand at this podium tonight.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:52.100
This is the same dinner where Alan Greenspan
warned of irrational exuberance.

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:58.019
This is the same dinner where Charles Krauthammer
described an American foreign policy for a

00:14:58.019 --> 00:14:59.350
unipolar world.

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:06.089
And of course, it’s the dinner where AEI
interns eat for free.

00:15:06.089 --> 00:15:14.420
That’s so great.

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:18.200
And I really want to thank all of you for
showing up tonight.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:26.860
AEI had the wisdom to schedule this dinner
on the same night as Game 6 of the World Series.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:32.069
They really wanted to test how many of you
were dedicated to public policy.

00:15:32.069 --> 00:15:38.500
I will forgive those of you who decide to
check your phones for the score, like my husband

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.040
over here.

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:46.199
It’s a tremendous honor to receive an award
named for the great Irving Kristol, who did

00:15:46.199 --> 00:15:52.730
so much to strengthen the intellectual foundations
that led to American victory in the Cold War

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:56.009
and freedom for hundreds of millions of people.

00:15:56.009 --> 00:16:02.899
For most of the past 15 years, I have lived
and worked where AEI’s ideas are tested

00:16:02.899 --> 00:16:04.870
in practice.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:12.260
In South Carolina, we led the way on opening
markets, reducing regulations, reforming education,

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:15.110
and moving people from welfare to work.

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:23.160
For those of us engaged in conservative public
policy, AEI is a trusted source.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:28.300
You don’t have to agree with all of its
ideas, but you can be sure they are all deeply

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:31.709
rooted in America’s values.

00:16:31.709 --> 00:16:36.899
You can have confidence they exist to preserve
what President Lincoln called “the last

00:16:36.899 --> 00:16:39.670
best hope of earth.”

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:44.350
I want to start by saying I am optimistic
about America’s future.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:50.710
I know that for those who spend too much time
on Twitter, it may at times look like it’s

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:54.800
all over for the American experiment.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:59.180
Depending on which cable network you prefer,
there is a sense that things are coming off

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:01.819
the rails in America today.

00:17:01.819 --> 00:17:05.160
We have our problems; there’s no denying
that.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:13.140
But America has survived a revolution, a foreign
invasion, a civil war, a great depression,

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:20.020
the largest war in world history, and social
unrest of a dozen different stripes, and we’ve

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:23.640
come out stronger after every test.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.830
We are a truly exceptional nation.

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:33.240
One reason things may seem dark, especially
on social media and cable news, is that we

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:39.610
have lots of people who are attempting to
reject the very things that set America apart.

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:46.260
This is a real problem, and it’s one we
should strongly resist.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.680
Our history is being attacked as a lie.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:57.180
Influential voices on the left claim America
was founded not in freedom, but in oppression.

00:17:57.180 --> 00:18:03.730
The idea that we must be in control of our
borders is dismissed as uncaring bigotry.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:08.610
Some are attempting to redefine American citizenship
itself.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:14.970
Ronald Reagan once asked Americans to choose
between being citizens or subjects.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:19.780
It was the ’60s; government and liberalism
were on the march.

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:22.920
The question fit the times.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.420
Today we face a different choice.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:30.530
It is a choice between citizenship and victimhood.

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:36.010
Our politics is becoming a contest over who’s
got the biggest grievance, who’s getting

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:42.150
the short end of the stick, who’s being
taken advantage of by rigged system.

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:48.320
Back then, Reagan pointed to big government
as the enemy, and he was right.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:55.170
Today, Americans increasingly see each other
as the enemy, and that’s not right.

00:18:55.170 --> 00:19:02.820
Too many regard those who disagree with them
not simply as wrong but as evil, but it’s

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:08.030
hard to have government by the people and
for the people if we regard each other as

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:12.460
unworthy of even having a conversation with.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:18.390
You see this a lot on college campuses, but
you even see it at restaurants these days.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:24.880
I can’t believe I’m saying this, but sometimes
it even makes me miss what passes for moral

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:29.490
clarity at the United Nations.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:35.770
My distinguished predecessor and an AEI alumna
Jeane Kirkpatrick said her experience at the

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:43.860
UN left her “in every way a sadder and wiser
woman about the world.”

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:50.810
While I rarely disagree with Ambassador Kirkpatrick,
we do partially differ on this.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:57.000
My time at the UN certainly made me wiser
about the world and sadder about parts of

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:02.300
it, but it also made me more grateful about
our country.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:07.080
At the UN, I worked alongside the ambassadors
of dictators and strongmen.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:14.240
I traveled to places most Americans will never
go, and I saw things most Americans will never

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.870
see.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:20.900
What I saw cut through the loud and polarizing
voices in our country.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:28.380
I saw what sets America apart, what we must
protect and preserve.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:33.940
When you’ve heard a South Sudanese refugee
woman tell you about watching soldiers throw

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:41.920
her baby into a fire, when you’ve seen Venezuelan
families walk for hours in the blazing sun

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:47.790
to receive the one meal they might have that
day, when you’ve negotiated with representatives

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:54.620
of a Chinese regime that is building a surveillance
state that would horrify Orwell, when you’ve

00:20:54.620 --> 00:21:00.840
seen and done these things, you see just how
profound the gifts are that we have received

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:03.070
as Americans.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.660
And they’re not just for Americans.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:10.880
When the cameras were off at the UN, ambassadors
from all parts of the world made it clear

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:16.410
to me that they envy our ability to live and
speak freely.

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:18.640
They admire our principles.

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:25.180
And they depend on us to lead the world in
accordance with our values.

00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:30.960
At times, this is a much harder case to make
among Americans than among nations.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:37.720
We are too caught up in our differences, whether
socioeconomic, racial, or religious.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:42.290
These differences increasingly define our
politics.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.080
To a certain extent, they should.

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:50.270
We are very different people, and there are
important differences between the political

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:51.270
parties.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:53.570
We shouldn’t gloss over those differences.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:55.670
We should debate them vigorously.

00:21:55.670 --> 00:22:03.720
But when we retreat into identity and grievance
politics, we make the choice for victimhood

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:06.140
over citizenship.

00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:12.760
By constantly blaming others, we reject personal
responsibility for ourselves, our families,

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.930
and our communities.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:22.110
The choice of victimhood takes us further
away from our exceptionalism, but we can still

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:26.430
be optimistic about our future as an exceptional
nation.

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.310
And here’s why.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:35.600
Ninety-three years ago, on the 150th anniversary
of the signing of the Declaration of Independence,

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:44.601
President Calvin Coolidge said, “Amid all
the clash of conflicting interests, amid all

00:22:44.601 --> 00:22:52.370
the welter of partisan politics, every American
can turn for solace and consolation to the

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:58.230
Declaration of Independence and the Constitution
of the United States.”

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:02.820
That statement is just as fitting today.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.160
Equality before the law.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:06.420
Freedom.

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:07.700
Inalienable rights.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:09.670
These are not obstructions.

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:14.280
They are our inheritance as human beings.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.420
The Declaration of Independence outlined them.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:21.910
The Constitution established them as a system
of government.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:26.960
It is these sacred principles that make me
optimistic.

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:31.480
They anchor our nation, and they show us the
way forward.

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:36.420
For instance, our immigration system is truly
broken.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:40.050
It is no longer controlled by the rule of
law.

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:46.860
It doesn’t acknowledge, much less emphasize,
the values that make us Americans.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:53.780
The American people are justifiably concerned
by both the security of our borders and by

00:23:53.780 --> 00:24:00.400
America’s place as a home for those with
the dream and drive to breathe free.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:06.820
People from all over the world are drawn to
the United States by our exceptionalism, our

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:11.820
freedom, our opportunity, and our belief in
human dignity.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.250
My parents were among them.

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:20.340
They came from India to rural South Carolina
in the 1960s.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:23.070
My mother wore a sari.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:24.910
My father wore a turban.

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:27.480
He still does to this day.

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:28.870
We were different.

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:35.150
We stood out, and my family felt the pain
of being judged by our difference.

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:37.870
Many Americans have felt this pain.

00:24:37.870 --> 00:24:39.690
Many have felt much worse.

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:44.050
But my parents refused to let it define them.

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:48.090
They chose citizenship over victimhood.

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:50.830
They came to the United States legally.

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:56.470
They respected the right of the American people
to protect their sovereignty, to be the ones

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:59.110
who decide who can join us.

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:04.010
And in return, America welcomed them.

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:08.140
Immigration is a source of American strength
when it is conducted in accordance with our

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:13.090
principles, but it must be a two-way street.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:18.120
We welcome immigrants who come to America
in accordance with the rule of law, and we

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:24.820
must call upon those immigrants to embrace
our values and respect our laws in order to

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:28.080
become Americans.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:32.650
American leadership in the world is also challenged
by some today.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:39.150
To remain strong, they say, America must give
up its advocacy of freedom and human dignity

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:40.820
abroad.

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:44.380
This represents a failure of memory.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:50.650
A world without United States leadership does
not mean a world in which our values and interests

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:54.170
peacefully coexist with other countries.

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:58.890
If it meant countries could live peacefully
pursuing their interests, there would be more

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:02.780
to recommend the United States retreating
from the world.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:09.220
Instead, it means a world with Chinese values
devoted to Chinese interests.

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:13.750
It means a world with Russian values devoted
to Russian interests.

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:18.120
It means a world in which radical Islam runs
rampant.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:25.510
That is a dark place for American values and
a dangerous place for American security.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:30.890
It would be nicer and cheaper if American
leadership was not needed to protect our values

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:32.610
and our interests.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.730
Sadly, that’s not the world we live in.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:40.710
If you don’t believe me, I invite you to
spend a day in the United Nations Security

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.690
Council.

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:48.800
Some of its powerful members not only don’t
share American values; they actively work

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:50.580
to defeat them.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:57.750
We faced a choice, either sacrifice our values
in an attempt to win friends at the UN or

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:03.330
defend our values and fight some fights virtually
alone.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:07.260
Freedom and human dignity aren’t just the
right values to promote.

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:14.070
They are our most powerful foreign policy
tools, which is why we fought for them.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:18.570
We called out Russia for its violations of
Ukraine’s sovereignty.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:24.360
We sanctioned the North Korean regime for
representing a unique evil in the world, a

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:30.770
dictatorship that is starving its own people
to finance the creation of nuclear weapons.

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:37.460
And on Israel, after a heartbreaking betrayal
by the previous administration, we once again

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:44.550
embraced our friend and ally.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:56.330
At the UN, Israel is the clearest example
of this worldwide clash of values.

00:27:56.330 --> 00:28:03.260
The only fully democratic country in the Middle
East is routinely demonized as an oppressor.

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:08.530
The only country in the region that represents
religious freedom, gender equality, and gay

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:16.400
rights is singled out for abuse in the tragically
misnamed UN Human Rights Council.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:23.140
If America does not lead the way in stopping
this nonsense, no one else will.

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:27.460
Here at home, the presidential election season
is upon us.

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:33.230
With that comes the inevitable running to
the partisan barricades, and that’s okay.

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:36.050
Electing a president is serious business.

00:28:36.050 --> 00:28:39.920
The two sides have very different visions
for our country.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:47.010
We deserve a spirited campaign to debate those
differences, but here again, respect for the

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:51.210
Constitution should guide us.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:54.070
President Trump is a disruptor.

00:28:54.070 --> 00:28:59.530
That makes some people very happy, and it
makes some people very mad.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:05.200
But if we are a country that lives by the
rule of law, we must all accept that we have

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:11.880
one president at a time and that president
attained his office by the choice of the American

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:23.440
people.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:28.140
When I was in the administration, I served
alongside colleagues who believe that the

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:34.490
best thing to do for America was to undermine
and obstruct the president.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:38.630
Some wrote about it anonymously in The New
York Times.

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:40.830
Others just did it.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:44.340
They sincerely believed they were doing the
right thing.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:48.220
I sincerely believed they weren’t.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:54.140
The president was the choice of the people
in accordance with our founding charter.

00:29:54.140 --> 00:30:00.790
No policy disagreement with him, no matter
how heartfelt, justifies undermining the lawful

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:05.490
authority that is vested in his office by
the Constitution.

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:10.790
For those who don’t like the president,
they are free to protest him, and many do.

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:16.680
But if you serve in the administration, you
are not free to push your personal agenda.

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:20.890
What’s at stake is not President Trump’s
policies.

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:25.330
What’s at stake is the Constitution.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:39.740
We are a nation with a history, borders, and
common language.

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:44.000
We are also the first nation devoted to a
higher good.

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.950
Our beliefs are what make us Americans.

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:53.640
Our Constitution is what allows this great
experiment in democracy to survive and thrive.

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:55.580
AEI gets this.

00:30:55.580 --> 00:31:00.860
You are working to restore respect for the
Constitution and American life.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:15.120
Congratulations on your new research division
for social, cultural, and constitutional studies.

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:18.960
Another reason I’m optimistic is because
I’ve been spending time with high school

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.370
and college students.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:27.310
Most often they face hostility on their campuses
because of their conservative views, but they

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:29.230
are undaunted.

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:34.210
They want to be leaders, specifically American
leaders.

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:37.360
They appreciate the things that make America
exceptional.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:45.070
The more time I spend with these young people,
the more confident I am about our future.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:49.290
These young Americans are not complacent,
far from it.

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:54.710
They understand that our ability to speak
freely, to debate, to worship and determine

00:31:54.710 --> 00:32:01.650
our own destinies, these are things to be
grateful for and preserve.

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:07.830
It was 1862 when Abraham Lincoln called America
“the last best hope of earth.”

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:13.060
Our country was more deeply divided than ever
before or since.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:18.540
It was a time of war for the country and a
time of political peril for Lincoln.

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:22.870
Still, he showed confidence in America’s
future.

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:29.360
In his message, Lincoln asked repeatedly,
“Can we do better?”

00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:35.440
He answers his own question by reminding his
countrymen and women of America’s higher

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:40.080
purpose and greater gifts: our exceptionalism.

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:46.520
I can’t match the wisdom and eloquence of
Lincoln, but like him, I believe in the American

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:51.750
people’s ability to find our way through
any challenge by honoring the beliefs that

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:52.920
define us.

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:58.970
There will always be work to do to be a more
perfect union, but we will always do better

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:04.180
if we acknowledge the truth that the world
already knows about us.

00:33:04.180 --> 00:33:09.620
Even on our worst days, we are blessed to
be Americans.

00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:16.040
Thank you very much.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:16.820
God bless.

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:18.120
Thank you.

